        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The final flight of the Gemini program, Gemini 12 (G-12), was another flight where the skills needed for a moon landing during the following Apollo program were practiced.  Almost four days in length, the mission included rendezvous and docking maneuvers and 10 scheduled science experiments.  Astronauts on this flight were Command Pilot James A. Lovell, Jr. and Pilot Edwin E. Aldrin, Jr..<P>No human life sciences experiments were conducted on G-12, but as with all previous Gemini flights, continuous, real-time physiological information on all crewmembers was monitored via telemetered data and voice transmissions.  Data was provided by a two-lead electrocardiogram, an impedance pneumograph measuring respiration, an oral thermistor measuring body temperature, and a blood pressure measuring system.  All of this hardware, including signal conditioners, was mounted on a biosensor harness that fit under the astronaut's flight suit.  Additionally, some inflight film footage was utilized for postflight assessment of crew health.<P>Only one biological experiment was performed during the Gemini 12 mission.  This investigation was conducted in the discipline of developmental biology.  The Frog Egg Growth experiment''s objectives were to determine the effect of microgravity on the ability of the fertilized frog egg (eggs of the bullfrog <I>Rana pipiens</I>)  to divide normally, and to differentiate and form a normal embryo.  This experiment was a reflight of the Gemini 8 experiment, and, while successful, was not conclusive in that the eggs were fertilized before flight and were exposed to microgravity only after the two-cell stage.<P>Several types of photography experiments were conducted during Gemini 12.  Synoptic Terrain Photography was devoted to the study of the Earth terrain, and produced numerous useful pictures usable for geology, geography or oceanography.  Synoptic Weather Photography documented many weather patterns and events for meteorological studies. <P> The Airglow Horizon Photography experiment photographed the nighttime airglow layer on Earth.  Airglow is caused by any light sources reflected in the Earth''s atmosphere, including city lights and light reflecting off the moon.  The objective of the Airglow photography experiment was to obtain worldwide measurements of airglow altitude and intensity.  The Dim Sky Photography experiment also obtained pictures of the airglow phenomenon.<P>The Ultraviolet Astronomical Photography experiment obtained ultraviolet photographs of stars that had before been possible only from rocket flights.  Since the spacecraft windows did not admit ultraviolet light, the experiment would not have been possible without the EVA capability of the astronauts.  During the three trials of this experiment on Gemini flights, considerable ultraviolet information was obtained and was especially useful in planning future ultraviolet experiments for manned flights.<P>Other science experiments intended to passively collect data on micrometeorites while attached outside the spacecraft and exposed to open space.  During the G-12 mission, the extravehicular pilot opened the package on the Gemini 12 target vehicle and exposed the sensitive collection plates to the space environment.  Another micrometeorite collection package was exposed for 6 hours on the exterior of the G-12 spacecraft.   Any microscopic meteorite impact craters left on the collection surface were analyzed to assess potential harm that may be caused by micrometeorite damage to spacecraft or spacesuits during EVAs.<P>The Dim Sky Photography experiment and the Daytime Sodium Cloud investigation were not completed due to equipment malfunctions.  Also, the Celestial Radiometry experiment was not completed due to time conflicts, i.e., interference by other mission activities critical to the objectives of the flight.  Nevertheless, the majority of scientific experiments were completed successfully and lead the way for the next era of space flight:  lunar exploration.<END>
